// EXPECT_STDOUT got=42
//
// Registry 221.1 #567, the shape that actually reddens.
//
// The peer fixture p567_bare_pattern_resolves_by_type.nv is the acceptance's
// "two enums sharing a variant name". It is a good statement of intent and a
// poor judge: with several sums declaring `Done` in one module, the old
// first-wins lookup happened to pick a right-enough one, so that file stays
// green with the fix disabled. A fixture that cannot redden proves nothing,
// so the defect gets its own file, in the reported shape.
//
// Here exactly ONE user sum declares `Done`, and it is generic. First-wins
// then reached past it to `OnceState.Done` -- a sum hardcoded in nova_rt and
// present in every program with no import at all. The arm compared a foreign
// tag, missed BOTH arms, and `x` came out of uninitialised stack memory:
// `got=4` for an input of 42, silently.
//
// Kill-switch NOVA_KILL_567=1 restores that on the same binary, and this file
// goes red -- which is the whole reason it exists next to its peer.
module standalone.p567_variant_name_collides_with_runtime_sum

type P567Outcome[T] enum Done(T) | Cancelled

fn unwrap(out P567Outcome[int]) -> int {
    match out {
        Done(x) => x
        Cancelled => -1
    }
}

fn main() Io -> () {
    println("got=${unwrap(Done(42))}")
}
